    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d281">

                <pb facs="0280" n="268" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg268"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/17886: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Thurston</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-12-18T10:00:00+03:00">December 18, 1941—10 a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received December 19—6:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2077. The Counselor of the British Embassy<anchor n="34" xml:id="d281-f1"/>
                    informed me last night that Sikorski, acting on reports of impending American,
                    British and Soviet discussions, has addressed a message to Stalin asserting the
                    claim of Poland to participate. His claim apparently was based upon, among other
                    considerations, the fact that Poland was among the first victims of Hitler’s war
                    of aggression and the opinion “that Poland is the touchstone of the probity of
                    the ideology of the Allies[”].</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Thurston</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d281-f1" n="34" xml:id="d281-f1-content">
                <p>Herbert Lacy Baggallay, Acting Counselor of the British Embassy.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
